<h2>What to do after a storm in Kyle</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not drive through flooded crossings on roads like Bunton Ln., and stay off wet or damaged roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before cleanup.</strong> Capture every side of the house, the fence line, vents, gutters, cars, and hailstones next to a coin or ruler.</li>
<li><strong>Make temporary repairs.</strong> Tarp openings and dry out wet areas to prevent further damage, and keep every receipt.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down the claim number and each adjuster’s name and contact details.</li>
<li><strong>Save the weather record</strong> for your date of loss. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through the rest.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Permits and building codes in Kyle</h2>
<p>The City of Kyle runs its own permit office, separate from the City of San Marcos Permit Center, and its permit and code requirements may differ. Before a roof replacement or structural repair, check with the City of Kyle about what it requires. If your property is outside city limits in unincorporated Hays County, note that the county does not issue residential building permits, though it does regulate septic, development, and floodplain matters.</p>
<p>Code requirements do not automatically mean your insurer pays for upgrades. That depends on your policy’s ordinance or law coverage, which is often limited. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes guide</a> explains how codes and claims interact.</p>

